            <ArticleTitle>Posterior wall capture and resultant common femoral occlusion complicating StarClose access closure.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>469-71</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.jvs.2008.03.013</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Vascular closure devices have been shown to decrease hemostasis and ambulation time after percutaneous transcatheter procedures. The StarClose Vascular Closure System (Abbott Vascular, Redwood City, Calif) is a shape memory clip-mediated device that is designed to provide extraluminal mechanical closure without compromising the arterial lumen. We report a case of iatrogenic arterial occlusion secondary to mechanical closure of the anterior and posterior walls of the common femoral artery with a StarClose device. We provide objective observations from the rescue surgical intervention and discuss potential mechanisms of device failure.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
